    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The discovery of oil in Arctic regions led to studies of the influence of permafrost on Alaska's North Slope drilling and completions. An important aspect of this problem is the thawing of permafrost around an oil well, because this thawing can create unstable conditions. In designing the well to avoid damage to the well and to the environment, it is necessary to have an accurate forecast of the amount of thawing.The solution of this problem involves the tracking of a moving boundary between the thawed and frozen regions where the phase change takes place. This problem is known as the Stefan problem in the heat conduction field, and similar moving boundary problems occur in many other fields.Numerical methods have been more successful than analytic techniques in treating this nonlinear problem. Three numerical methods of solution for the thawing of permafrost around an oil well are proposed in this study. Two methods are for one-dimensional and one is for two-dimensional Stefan problems in cylindrical systems. One method is extensively tested and is found to be efficient, providing accurate results for one-dimensional, two-phase Stefan problems.This method is used to generate solutions for radial thawing of permafrost with axial symmetry, where the solution comprises the temperature distributions in the thawed and frozen regions and the radius of the thawed region. The solution to this problem is a function of three dimensionless parameters, plus the dimensionless radius and dimensionless time.Solutions were generated for the range of values of the three parameters typically found in Alaska's North Slope. It is found that the relationship between the dimensionless radius of the thawed permafrost region and dimensionless time can be expressed by a simple power law equation containing two constants. The relationships among these two constants and the three dimensionless parameters are given in two correlating curves. Given the physical data for any well in a permafrost region, one can use these correlations to forecast the radius of the thawed permafrost region and its temperature distribution at any given time.The same solution can be applied with efficiency to insulated wells by using an effective radius which takes into account the thermal resistance near and in the wellbore.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
